     <actual value="Within the VIA Water trajectory, we assessed the product-market fit of the jerrycan filter in Ethiopia and Kenya. Based on the newly gained insights discussed in the previous pages, the following conclusions can be made on the match with the target customer (I), current design (II), and business case (IIII).   

The target customers are benefitted by a water filter at household level, greatly improving the quality of drinking water. However, with insufficient ability and willingness to pay (which equals less than half of the expected retail price), households are simply unable to purchase a filter, which complicates the business model and sales strategy.

 The product was generally well-received, as participants enjoyed using JERRY and experienced health benefits after usage. However, the dependency of local jerry cans proves challenging, as the product requires an external jerrycan in the current design.  

 We developed and tested different methods to bridge the gap between ability to pay and retail price, including micro-financing, carbon credits and CSR donation. We see potential in these alternative income streams as add-on to our regular business model, but challenges remain on the stability and time-efficiency, in particular on the short term."></actual>
